## Recording decisions

Use the cheapest record that works. Most decisions stop at 1 or 2.

1. The commit body. This is the default home for "why".
2. A `// why:` comment at the site, for a local choice a reader would undo.
3. A rule in this file, when it must change how every future session works.
   Propose it under "Needs owner"; the owner adds it.
4. An ADR, only when all three hold: it is costly to reverse (data model,
   public interface, vendor or platform, security posture); a real alternative
   was rejected; a future reader would reopen it without the reasoning.

Never write an ADR on your own initiative. Add a one-line proposal under
"Needs owner" in `STATUS.md` and write it only when the owner says yes. Expect a
handful per project, not one per feature. The bar and the steps are in
`docs/adr/README.md`; its index is usually all you need to read.

## Commits & branches

- Conventional Commits: `feat(scope):`, `fix:`, `docs:`, `test:`, `chore:`, `ci:`.
  Subject ≤ 72 chars, imperative; body explains *why* (the diff shows what).
- The commit body is this project's history. Say what was done and what was
  deferred; nothing else records it.
- One unit of work per PR where possible. Delete branches after merge.

## Code conventions

- Strict typing everywhere. No escape hatches (`any`, `# type: ignore`) without a
  `// why:` comment — the comment, not the linter, is the gate.
- Validate all external data at the boundary (schema-parse payloads, not trust).
- Prefer leaving a clear seam + a TODO referencing the plan doc over guessing.

## When you're unsure

Add the question under "Needs owner" in `STATUS.md` and stop. Do not imply
something exists when it is unbuilt.
